slug: writing-imrad-abstract-to-discussion
title: Writing IMRaD — From Abstract to Discussion
area: Research & Evidence
level: Foundation
best: Second year onwards
minutes: 12
hook: Good research badly written is invisible. IMRaD (Introduction, Methods, Results and Discussion) is the standard shape of a scientific paper because it answers four questions in order: why did you do it, what did you do, what did you find and what does it mean? Learn to write each part in plain, honest language. || Achhi research jo kharab likhi gayi ho nazar nahi aati. IMRaD (Introduction, Methods, Results aur Discussion) science paper ki mayaari shakal hai kyunke yeh chaar sawalon ka tarteeb se jawab deti hai: aap ne kyun kiya, kya kiya, kya paya aur is ka kya matlab hai? Har hissa saade, imaandar alfaaz mein likhna seekhein.
goal: By the end, you'll structure a paper in IMRaD form, write a structured abstract, state results without interpreting them, build a balanced discussion with limitations, and revise for clarity. || End tak aap paper ko IMRaD shakal mein tarteeb denge, munazzam abstract likhenge, nateeje tafseer kiye baghair bayan karenge, hadon ke saath mutawazin discussion banayenge, aur wazahat ke liye nazar-e-saani karenge.
say: **IMRaD** gives each part one job. **Introduction**: the problem, what is known, the gap and your aim (a funnel from broad to specific, ending with the question). **Methods**: enough detail for someone to repeat the study (design, participants, intervention, outcomes, analysis, ethics). **Results**: what you found, in the order of your methods, without opinions. **Discussion**: what the main result means, how it fits with other studies, limitations and conclusions. The **abstract** summarises all four in 250 to 300 words, and is often the only part read. || **IMRaD** har hissay ko ek kaam deta hai. **Introduction**: masla, kya maloom hai, kami aur aap ka maqsad (wasi se khaas tak funnel, sawal par khatam). **Methods**: itni tafseel ke koi study dohra sake (design, shirkat karne wale, intervention, nateeje, tajziya, ethics). **Results**: jo paya, methods ki tarteeb mein, bina raye ke. **Discussion**: asal nateeja ka kya matlab hai, doosri studies se kaise mel khata hai, hadein aur nateeja. **Abstract** chaaron ka 250 se 300 alfaaz mein khulasa hai, aur aksar wohi hissa parha jata hai.
analogy: Think of **telling a patient's story to a colleague**. First, why you are talking about this patient (introduction). Then what you did (methods). Then what happened (results). Then what you think it means and what you would do next (discussion). If you mix them, your colleague gets confused. IMRaD keeps them separate. || **Mareez ki kahani saathi ko sunana** sochein. Pehle aap is mareez ke baare mein kyun baat kar rahe hain (introduction). Phir aap ne kya kiya (methods). Phir kya hua (results). Phir aap ke khayal mein is ka kya matlab hai aur aage kya karenge (discussion). Agar mila dein to saathi ulajh jata hai. IMRaD in ko alag rakhta hai.
compare: What belongs where, and what does not || Kya kahan aata hai, aur kya nahi
head: Section || Hissa ;; What belongs || Kya aata hai ;; What does NOT belong || Kya NAHI aata
row: Introduction || Introduction ;; The problem, a short summary of what is known, the gap and the aim || Masla, jo maloom hai uska mukhtasar khulasa, kami aur maqsad ;; Your results, or a history of the entire field || Aap ke nateeje, ya poore shobay ki tareekh
row: Methods || Methods ;; Design, participants, intervention, outcomes, analysis, ethics, registration || Design, shirkat karne wale, intervention, nateeje, tajziya, ethics, registration ;; Results, or opinions about the methods || Nateeje, ya methods ke baare mein raye
row: Results || Results ;; Participant flow, baseline data, main outcome with effect size and interval, secondary outcomes, harms || Shirkat karne walon ka flow, baseline data, asal nateeja effect size aur interval ke saath, secondary nateeje, nuqsan ;; Explanations or comparisons with other studies || Wazahaat ya doosri studies se muqabla
row: Discussion || Discussion ;; Main finding, meaning, comparison with others, strengths, limitations, implications, conclusion || Asal finding, matlab, doosron se muqabla, khoobiyan, hadein, asarat, nateeja ;; New results, or claims beyond the data || Naye nateeje, ya data se aage ke daawe
check: In which section should the explanation "the benefit may be due to increased attention" appear? || "Faida zyada tawajjo ki wajah se ho sakta hai" ki wazahat kis hissay mein aani chahiye?
ok: Discussion, because it is an interpretation of the results || Discussion, kyunke yeh nateejon ki tafseer hai || Yes — interpretation belongs in the discussion. || Haan — tafseer discussion mein aati hai.
no: Results || Results || Results should state facts, not interpretation. || Results mein haqaiq aate hain, tafseer nahi.
no: Methods || Methods || Methods describe what was done. || Methods batate hain kya kiya gaya.
check: A paper's abstract says "the treatment was effective", but the results show a 3-point improvement (95 percent CI -2 to 8) on a 100-point scale. What is the problem? || Paper ka abstract kehta hai "ilaaj mu'assir tha", lekin results 100 point scale par 3 point ki behtari (95 fisad CI -2 se 8) dikhate hain. Masla kya hai?
ok: The conclusion goes beyond the data: the interval includes no effect and the difference is small || Nateeja data se aage nikal gaya: interval mein koi asar na hona shamil hai aur farq chhota hai || Yes — conclusions must match the results. || Haan — nateeje results se milne chahiye.
no: Nothing, because 3 is positive || Kuch nahi, kyunke 3 musbat hai || The interval crosses zero. || Interval zero ko paar karta hai.
no: The abstract is too short || Abstract bohat chhota hai || Length is not the issue. || Lambai masla nahi.
check: Which is the most useful way to end the discussion? || Discussion khatam karne ka sab se mufeed tareeqa kaun sa hai?
ok: A short, honest conclusion that matches the results, states limitations and suggests what should be done or studied next || Mukhtasar, imaandar nateeja jo results se mile, hadein batao aur batao agle qadam par kya hona ya study hona chahiye || Yes — it respects the reader and the data. || Haan — yeh parhne wale aur data dono ki izzat hai.
no: A bold claim that the treatment should be adopted everywhere || Bohat bara daawa ke ilaaj har jagah apnana chahiye || That goes beyond the data. || Yeh data se aage hai.
no: A list of every detail again || Har tafseel ki list phir se || Repeating is not concluding. || Dohrana nateeja nahi.
mistake: **Burying the answer or overselling it.** A paper where you cannot find the main result in the abstract, or where the conclusion says more than the data, loses trust. State the question and main result near the start of the abstract, report negative results honestly and write a limitations paragraph that names the real weaknesses (small sample, short follow-up, no blinding), not just generic ones. || **Jawab dabana ya zyada bechna.** Aisa paper jis ke abstract mein asal nateeja nazar na aaye, ya jis ka nateeja data se zyada kahe, bharosa kho deta hai. Sawal aur asal nateeja abstract ke aaghaz ke qareeb batayein, manfi nateeje imaandari se report karein aur hadon ka paragraph likhein jo asal kamzoriyan (chhota sample, mukhtasar follow-up, blinding nahi) batayein, sirf aam nahi.
clinic: **Write short, plain sentences.** Aim for one idea per sentence, active voice and familiar words ("use" not "utilise"). Write the methods and results first, then the introduction and discussion, and the abstract last. Read your draft aloud, and ask a colleague outside your field to explain what they understood. In Pakistan, write in clear English and avoid long, complex sentences that translation tools mishandle. || **Chhote, saade jumle likhein.** Ek jumle mein ek khayal, active voice aur aam alfaaz ("use" na ke "utilise") ka maqsad rakhein. Pehle methods aur results likhein, phir introduction aur discussion, aur abstract aakhir mein. Apna draft zor se parhein, aur apne shobay se bahar ke saathi se poochhein unhon ne kya samjha. Pakistan mein saaf angrezi likhein aur lambay, pechida jumlon se bachein jinhein translation tools ghalat karte hain.
reveal: Why write the methods and results first? || Methods aur results pehle kyun likhein? || Because they are the facts of your study, and everything else grows from them. Once you know exactly what you did and found, the introduction becomes the story that leads to them and the discussion becomes the story that follows. Writing the abstract last means it reflects what the paper actually says, not what you planned. This order also reduces the temptation to write the story first and fit the data to it. || Kyunke yeh aap ke study ke haqaiq hain aur baqi sab in se nikalta hai. Jab aap theek theek jaan lein ke aap ne kya kiya aur paya, to introduction woh kahani ban jati hai jo in tak le jati hai aur discussion woh jo is ke baad aati hai. Abstract aakhir mein likhna usay wohi dikhata hai jo paper asal mein kehta hai, na ke jo aap ne plan kiya tha. Yeh tarteeb pehle kahani likh kar data ko us mein fit karne ki targhib bhi kam karti hai.
case: A student's first draft puts a table of results in the introduction, explains the methods in the discussion, and ends with "the treatment is the best". Her supervisor cannot find the main result. || The structure is mixed up, and the conclusion is overstated. || She moves each element into its section, writes a structured abstract with background, methods, results and conclusion, includes the confidence interval and a limitations paragraph. || The revised paper is clear, passes peer review with minor changes and is published.
safety: **Do not overstate benefit or hide harm.** The discussion must report adverse events and uncertainties. If a technique may be risky, say what is known and not known. Papers shape practice: an overstated conclusion could lead other clinicians to use a treatment unsafely. Also protect patients' privacy in tables, figures and photos. || **Faida ko mubalghe se bayan na karein aur nuqsan na chhupayein.** Discussion mein adverse events aur be-yaqeeniyan report honi chahiyen. Agar technique khatarnak ho sakti hai to batayein kya maloom hai aur kya nahi. Papers practice ko dhalte hain: mubalghe wala nateeja doosre clinicians ko ilaaj ghair mehfooz tareeqe se istemal karwa sakta hai. Tables, figures aur tasveeron mein mareezon ki nijiyat bhi mehfooz rakhein.
evidence: A survey of the scientific literature over fifty years showed that the IMRaD structure became the dominant format of research articles in biomedical journals from the 1940s, reaching almost universal use by the 1980s, because it mirrors the logic of scientific investigation and helps readers find information quickly. Guidance on scientific writing recommends writing the methods and results first and the abstract last, and stressing that conclusions must be supported by the data presented. || Pachaas saal ke science literature ke ek jaiza ne dikhaya ke IMRaD ka dhancha 1940s se biomedical journals mein research articles ki hawi shakl ban gaya, 1980s tak taqreeban aalmi istemal tak pohancha, kyunke yeh science ki tehqiq ke mantiq ko dikhata hai aur parhne walon ko maloomat jaldi dhoondhne mein madad deta hai. Science likhne ki rehnumai methods aur results pehle aur abstract aakhir mein likhne ki sifarish karti hai, aur zor deti hai ke nateeje pesh kiye gaye data se mutabiq hone chahiyen.
memory: **Why, How, What, So what.** Introduction (why), Methods (how), Results (what), Discussion (so what). || **Kyun, Kaise, Kya, Phir kya.** Introduction (kyun), Methods (kaise), Results (kya), Discussion (phir kya).
summary: IMRaD answers four questions in order: why, how, what and so what. || IMRaD chaar sawalon ka tarteeb se jawab deta hai: kyun, kaise, kya aur phir kya.
summary: Keep facts in Results and interpretation in Discussion, with honest limitations and conclusions that match the data. || Haqaiq Results mein aur tafseer Discussion mein rakhein, imaandar hadon aur data se milte nateejon ke saath.
summary: Write methods and results first, the abstract last, in plain sentences, and report harms and uncertainties. || Methods aur results pehle, abstract aakhir mein, saade jumlon mein likhein, aur nuqsan aur be-yaqeeniyan report karein.
card: What does IMRaD stand for? || IMRaD ka matlab kya hai? || Introduction, Methods, Results and Discussion. || Introduction, Methods, Results aur Discussion.
card: Where does interpretation belong? || Tafseer kahan aati hai? || In the Discussion. || Discussion mein.
card: What should an abstract summarise? || Abstract kya khulasa karta hai? || The background, methods, results and conclusion. || Background, methods, results aur nateeja.
card: What should a limitations paragraph name? || Hadon ka paragraph kya batata hai? || The real weaknesses, such as small sample or short follow-up. || Asal kamzoriyan, jaise chhota sample ya mukhtasar follow-up.
deeper: Reading a paper efficiently and spotting spin || /learn/reading-a-paper-efficiently-spotting-spin || What readers look for in your paper. || Parhne walon ko aap ke paper mein kya dhoondhte hain.
deeper: Reporting-guidelines map || /learn/reporting-guidelines-map-equator || The checklists that shape each section. || Checklists jo har hissay ko shakl deti hain.
source: Sollaci LB, Pereira MG. The introduction, methods, results, and discussion (IMRAD) structure: a fifty-year survey. J Med Libr Assoc 2004;92:364-367. || The introduction methods results and discussion IMRAD structure a fifty-year survey Sollaci Pereira
source: Hall GM, editor. How to write a paper. 5th ed. Chichester: Wiley-Blackwell; 2012. || How to write a paper Hall BMJ Books
